its origins in England, where the Victorian upper class developed a new parlour game to be played after dinner. It wasn’t quite as established as it is now, and was played with a stack of books marking the net, and books also used as rackets. The ball used was actually a golf ball."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"9cdf792ebd99","_type":"block","asset":null,"children":[{"_key":"40a1e2100bcf","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"In the UK, it had a number of different names, including ping pong and the appropriately British whiff whaff. The former was eventually trademarked by one game producer, which meant that table tennis became the standard name that was used throughout the English-speaking world."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"07177f234ef7","_type":"block","asset":null,"children":[{"_key":"6fbabb950108","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"The English Table Tennis Association was established in 1921, which set in motion the codification of the sport globally. The International Table Tennis Federation was set up five years later, and the following year saw the first-ever World Championships."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"6cbd9d173311","_type":"block","asset":null,"children":[{"_key":"0fcd23a81966","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"The sport continued to increase in popularity as it spread across the globe. Countries like China greatly enjoyed the sport, and this booming popularity saw the Olympics add it to their roster in 1988, which is how we find ourselves in a world where a former parlour game is now a legitimate sport."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"89bffe4567f2","_type":"block","asset":null,"children":[{"_key":"0b1c152b4c13","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"Table Tennis Rules & Basics of Ping Pong"}],"markDefs":[],"style":"h2"},{"_key":"375ad87a3d0f","_type":"block","asset":null,"children":[{"_key":"b0a7fae51643","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"Table tennis, as the name suggests, is essentially just "},{"_key":